        </div>
        <div class="media-card-body" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:center;">
          <p style="font-size:0.8125rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent);margin-bottom:0.5rem;">The Look</p>
          <h3 style="font-size:1.375rem;">Structured silhouettes, standing collars, rich brocade.</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.9375rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:0.75rem;">Ming Hanfu is the most tailored and formal of the dynastic styles. Cross-collar aoqun tops, standing-collared shirts, and heavy brocade give it a dignified, at-ease presence.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>What Defines the Ming Hanfu Look</h2>
      <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
        <li><strong>The mamianqun:</strong> A wrap skirt with pleated sides and flat, embroidered front/back panels. The defining Ming piece.</li>
        <li><strong>Standing collars:</strong> A subtle upright collar that frames the neck, adding dignity and structure.</li>
        <li><strong>Rich embroidery:</strong> Gold-wrapped thread creating dragons, phoenixes, peonies, clouds, and waves on the skirt panels.</li>
        <li><strong>Adjustable waist:</strong> Tied at the waist with fabric ties -- no zippers, no buttons. Forgiving and flexible.</li>
        <li><strong>Structured dignity:</strong> The overall effect is clean, composed, and authoritative -- traditional without being theatrical.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2rem;">Why Ming Is the Most Beginner-Friendly Hanfu</h2>
      <p class="text-muted">If you are buying your first Hanfu, the overwhelming consensus in the community is: start with Ming. Here is why:</p>
      <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
        <li><strong>Adjustable fit:</strong> The mamianqun ties at the waist, accommodating weight fluctuations and different body shapes in a way that fitted garments cannot.</li>
        <li><strong>Modern-friendly:</strong> A simple cotton mamianqun reads as elegant retro fashion, not costume. Pair it with a fitted modern top and it works in casual settings.</li>
        <li><strong>Mix-and-match:</strong> One mamianqun works with multiple jackets, and one ao jacket works with skirts in different colors. Your wardrobe expands exponentially.</li>
        <li><strong>Flattering on everyone:</strong> The structured A-line shape creates a defined waist and elongating vertical lines that work for tall, petite, slim, and curvy bodies alike.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2rem;">Key Ming Hanfu Garments You Need</h2>
      <div class="grid-2" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:1rem;">
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Mamianqun (Horse-Face Skirt)</h3>
          <p>Your essential first purchase. A pleated wrap skirt with flat panels. Start with a basic color (navy, black, dark green) in cotton for daily wear, then add silk brocade versions with embroidery for formal occasions.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Ao (Jacket)</h3>
          <p>A cross-collar or standing-collar jacket, hip-length, with wide or narrow sleeves. The classic top to pair with the mamianqun. A well-made ao can also be worn with modern skirts and trousers.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Bijia (Sleeveless Over-Jacket)</h3>
          <p>An optional layering piece: a sleeveless, side-buttoned vest worn over the ao. Adds visual complexity and warmth.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Embroidered Cloth Shoes</h3>
          <p>Flat-soled cloth shoes with embroidered toes. The classic Ming footwear. Neutral-colored flats also work for a modern touch.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

